//  29-8-2026 

/// Which region of the storefront a banner renders in. The three HERO_* values
/// address the homepage hero's visually distinct slots, which have different
/// aspect ratios and so cannot share one bucket:
///   HERO_SLIDER - the rotating panel (multiple banners cycle as slides)
///   HERO_SIDE   - the secondary tiles beside or beneath it
///   HERO_PROMO  - the remaining wide/third tile
///
/// THESE ARE SLOTS, NOT A LAYOUT. The hero's arrangement is a merchant setting
/// (`StoreSetting.homeConfig`, the HERO entry's `variant`), and each layout uses
/// a different subset of these three at different shapes:
///   SPLIT_THREE   SLIDER + 2x SIDE + 1x PROMO   (the default)
///   SPLIT_ONE     SLIDER + 1x SIDE
///   FULL_SLIDER   SLIDER only
///   SLIDER_STACK  SLIDER + 2x SIDE + 1x PROMO, stacked in a row beneath it
///
/// NO PLACEMENT IS EVER ADDED PER LAYOUT. Artwork belongs to the store, not to
/// an arrangement: a slot the current layout does not render is simply not read,
/// its rows stay exactly as they are, and it renders again unchanged if the
/// merchant switches back. Per-layout placements would instead make switching
/// layout look like it destroyed a merchant's uploads. See
/// openspec/changes/add-hero-section-variants, design.md Decision 6.
///
/// Kept in sync by hand with `bannerPlacementEnum` in banner.validation.ts.
/// Addressable storefront slot a Campaign can occupy, so the storefront can ask
/// "which campaign is in this slot?" without hardcoding a campaign id. Mirrors
/// how BannerPlacement below addresses hero regions.
///
/// FLASH_SALE is defined but rendered by no storefront section yet — it exists
/// so adding the second slot does not require another migration.
enum CampaignPlacement {
  DEAL_OF_WEEK
  FLASH_SALE
}

enum BannerPlacement {
  HEADER
  MID
  FOOTER
  SIDEBAR
  POPUP
  HERO_SLIDER
  HERO_SIDE
  HERO_PROMO
}

enum BannerType {
  IMAGE // full png/jpg, just clickable, no dynamic content
  DYNAMIC // title, price, discount, button etc dynamically shown on top of bg
}

/// How many tiles across a PromoBannerGroup renders on the storefront.
///
/// UNLIKE `HERO_VARIANTS` AND THE OTHER LAYOUT TUPLES, ORDER HERE IS NOT
/// LOAD-BEARING — those are TypeScript tuples whose position 0 IS the default,
/// so reordering them restyles every store that never opened the control. This
/// is a Prisma enum with an explicit `@default(THREE)` on the column, so the
/// default is named rather than positional and the members may be listed in any
/// order.
///
/// THREE is the default because three-across is what the storefront rendered
/// before promo banners were groupable at all — the same reasoning that pins
/// SPLIT_THREE and GRID at position 0 of their tuples. A store migrated from
/// before this change must look identical afterwards.
///
///   ONE     one full-width tile
///   TWO     two half-width tiles
///   THREE   three tiles across                                     (DEFAULT)
///
/// A GROUP'S LAYOUT IS INDEPENDENT OF HOW MANY BANNERS IT HOLDS. A THREE group
/// holding two banners renders two tiles; that is not an error state. Refusing
/// it would deadlock the merchant — they could not remove a banner without
/// first changing the layout, nor change the layout without first adding
/// banners. The admin warns; the API does not refuse. See
/// openspec/changes/add-promo-banner-groups, design.md Decision 4.
///
/// Kept in sync by hand with `PROMO_BANNER_LAYOUTS` in
/// store-setting.constant.ts and its mirrors in both frontends.
enum PromoBannerLayout {
  ONE
  TWO
  THREE
}

/// Only two states on purpose. Versioning, scheduled publishing and
/// drafts-alongside-live are explicitly out of scope for content pages — see
/// add-admin-ui-cms-section design.md, Non-Goals. DRAFT is indistinguishable
/// from "no such page" to a visitor.
enum PageStatus {
  DRAFT
  PUBLISHED
}

/// Two states, for the same reason PageStatus has two: a DRAFT post is
/// indistinguishable from "no such post" to a visitor, and scheduled publishing
/// is out of scope (see add-currency-format-and-home-content-cms design.md,
/// Non-Goals). `publishedAt` is the DATE ON THE CARD, authored by the merchant
/// — it is not what decides visibility, and a future-dated published post is
/// live now with a future date printed on it.
enum BlogPostStatus {
  DRAFT
  PUBLISHED
}

/// A post shows one thing, and this column says which.
///
/// Stated rather than inferred from which of `imageUrl` / `videoUrl` happens to
/// be non-null: with three nullable columns, "image or video, never both" is a
/// rule the data merely implies, and a row with both populated has no defined
/// answer. With this, it does. The Zod schema enforces that the URL columns
/// match whatever this says.
enum BlogMediaType {
  NONE
  IMAGE
  VIDEO
}

/// Two states, matching BlogPostStatus and PageStatus.
enum TestimonialStatus {
  DRAFT
  PUBLISHED
}

/// Which side of the amount the currency symbol sits on — `৳1,200.00` or
/// `1,200.00 ৳`.
///
/// A closed set of two, so the database says so rather than leaving a string
/// column that could hold "left", "LEFT" or "before". The spacing that goes
/// with each position is NOT a further setting: no space when the symbol leads,
/// one non-breaking space when it trails, which is what every leading-symbol
/// and every trailing-symbol locale does respectively. A knob whose right answer
/// is determined by the position it accompanies is a knob that is only ever set
/// wrong. See add-currency-format-and-home-content-cms design.md, Decision 3.
enum CurrencyPosition {
  BEFORE
  AFTER
}

/// Two states, matching PageStatus, BlogPostStatus and TestimonialStatus. A
/// DRAFT landing page is indistinguishable from "no such page" to a visitor —
/// the storefront's not-found page, not a 403, so an unpublished campaign
/// cannot be enumerated by anyone probing slugs.
///
/// Scheduled publishing is out of scope for the same reason it is for the other
/// three: a campaign starts when the merchant starts spending on ads, which is
/// not a time this system knows.
enum LandingPageStatus {
  DRAFT
  PUBLISHED
}

/// Whether the storefront root serves the full shop or a single campaign
/// landing page.
///
/// A closed set of two rather than a boolean `isLandingPageMode`, so the column
/// reads as what it is at every call site and a third mode (should one ever
/// exist) is an added variant rather than a second flag to keep consistent with
/// the first.
///
/// This governs the ROOT ONLY. In LANDING_PAGE mode every other storefront
/// route — catalogue, cart, checkout, tracking, account, CMS pages — stays live
/// and unchanged, so flipping the toggle breaks no existing link and is
/// reversible with no other consequence. See add-single-product-landing-page
/// design.md, Decision 6, and the `store-config/site-mode` spec.
enum SiteMode {
  WEBSITE
  LANDING_PAGE
}

/// How one of the storefront's brand slots — the header or the footer —
/// presents the shop's identity.
///
/// THE MODE DECIDES, NOT THE ARTWORK. A slot set to TEXT renders the wordmark
/// even when a logo is uploaded, which is the whole point: the header and the
/// footer are set independently, so a shop can show artwork on its brand-colour
/// header and the wordmark on its dark footer without deleting either image.
/// Inferring the mode from "is a logo uploaded" cannot express that — choosing
/// text for the footer would mean clearing the footer's artwork, which then
/// makes the header's logo fall through into the footer by the fallback in
/// StoreSetting.
///
/// A closed set of two rather than a boolean `headerShowsLogo`, for the reason
/// SiteMode above gives: a third variant (logo and wordmark side by side) would
/// be an added value rather than a second flag to keep consistent with the
/// first. Both columns default to TEXT, which is exactly what every storefront
/// rendered before this enum existed. See add-header-footer-brand-display
/// design.md, Decision 1.
enum BrandDisplayMode {
  TEXT
  LOGO
}

/// Whether a shopper takes delivery of an order or collects it themselves.
///
/// Recorded on the order rather than derived, because it decides what staff DO
/// with it: a collection order must not be handed to a courier. Before delivery
/// options existed this distinction was computed at checkout and then thrown
/// away, so an order gave no way to tell the two apart. See the
/// `commerce/delivery-options` spec, "The order records which option was
/// chosen".
enum DeliveryMethod {
  DELIVERY
  PICKUP
}

/// Which courier service the shop dispatches through.
///
/// EVERY VALUE HERE MUST HAVE A REGISTERED ADAPTER in
/// `src/app/module/courier/providers/`. The registry in `courier.provider.ts`
/// resolves this enum to an implementation and throws when it cannot — adding a
/// member without its adapter turns a dispatch into a runtime failure, so the
/// two changes belong in one commit.
///
/// `MANUAL` is a real provider, not the absence of one: it declares no
/// capabilities and creates no consignments, and it is what a merchant using a
/// courier this system does not integrate with selects. Modelling that as a
/// null selection instead would make "deliberately unintegrated" and
/// "misconfigured deployment" indistinguishable, which is exactly the
/// distinction the admin panel has to show.
///
/// See openspec/changes/add-courier-provider-selection, design.md Decisions 1
/// and 6.
enum CourierProvider {
  STEADFAST
  MANUAL
}
